aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1 has "□necessary_parts"; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.3 have "necessary_parts"; they differ when only "every animal has certain necessary parts: a sensorium, a mouth and belly to acquire and retain food, and parts to move from place to place" is true, which the second allows
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.3 with no modality; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1 as what is necessary or possible: its "should" or "must" does not say which it means
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "every animal has certain necessary parts: a sensorium, a mouth and belly to acquire and retain food, and parts to move from place to place" is true and "necessarily, every animal has certain necessary parts: a sensorium, a mouth and belly to acquire and retain food, and parts to move from place to place" is false. Is S2, "We admit that every city consists not of one, but many parts: thus, if we should endeavour to comprehend the different species of animals we should first of all note those parts which every animal must have, as a certain sensorium, and also what is necessary to acquire and retain food, as a mouth and a belly; besides certain parts to enable it to move from place to place.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1 has "only_parts ∧ parts_differ → □combos_species" and "parts_differ"; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.3 have "only_parts ∧ parts_differ → □combos_species"; they differ when only "these are the only parts of an animal" is true, which the second allows
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "these are the only parts of an animal" is true and "there are differences between these parts, in their various sorts of stomachs, bellies, sensoriums and motive powers", "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals", "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" and "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" are false. Is S3, "If, then, these are the only parts of an animal and there are differences between them; namely, in their various sorts of stomachs, bellies, and sensoriums: to which we must add their motive powers; the number of the combinations of all these must necessarily make up the different species of animals.", true then?
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1 has "¬◇great_diff_same_kind" and "¬◇great_diff_same_kind → combos_species"; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.3 have "¬◇great_diff_same_kind"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals", "possibly, the same kind of animal has a very great difference in its mouth or ears", "possibly, the same kind of animal has a very great difference in its mouth or ears", "possibly, the same kind of animal has a very great difference in its mouth or ears" and "possibly, the same kind of animal has a very great difference in its mouth or ears" are false. Is S4, "For it is not possible that the same kind of animal should have any very great difference in its mouth or ears; so that when all these are collected, who happen to have these things similar in all, they make up a species of animals of which there are as many as there are of these general combinations of necessary parts.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1 has "only_parts"; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r1.3 have "only_parts ∧ parts_differ"; they differ when only "these are the only parts of an animal" is true, which the first allows [in 1 of 2 runs]
